            <ArticleTitle>The Comfort app prototype: introducing a web-based application for monitoring comfort in palliative care.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>420-431</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.12968/ijpn.2017.23.9.420</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To introduce a web-based application for monitoring comfort in patients receiving palliative care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Multi-phase electronic application development process that concluded with a pilot design to assess the feasibility and acceptability of the developed app (n=7 patients).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The app is compatible with Android, iOS and Windows. The results from phases I and II provided the knowledge about monitoring comfort. In phase III, five experts analysed the content of the app. The assessment of comfort comprises 11 self-reported items (pain, tiredness, drowsiness, nausea, lack of appetite, shortness of breath, depression, anxiety, fear of the future, peace and the will to live). In phase IV, a total of 117 messages were retrieved. Participants considered the app simple, easy to use and useful.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This prototype is feasible and user-friendly. Further research is needed to continue the app development, particularly in terms of data protection.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
